title: The Psalms of David's Flight, Part 2 – The Hunted King
description: The Psalms of David's Flight, Part 2 of 2: the Ziphites betray David (Psalm 54) and David spares Saul in the cave at Engedi (Psalm 57), with the thread through all of David's flight, from the Psalm studies in the King James Bible.
output: lessons/bible/davids-flight-2-the-hunted-king.html

# The Psalms of David's Flight, part 2 of 2 (a Wednesday-night lesson, read from a laptop, about 30-35 minutes).
# Slide text is the author's own sentences, selected and set in the order of the 1 Samuel narrative, from:
#   Psalm 54 study (review/refile.json drive "psalms-54"), Psalm 57 study ("psalms-57", both halves: mercy, hope and
#   prayer; then the cave at Engedi), and for the closing thread the Psalm 56 ("psalms-56") and Psalm 59 ("psalms-59")
#   studies and the Psalm 34 series study (studies/data/books/Psalms.json, Psalms 34 main: 17834).
#   Verses typed in the studies are shown from the KJV.
# Layout: the pattern written at the top of davids-flight-1-the-flight-begins.deck.
# Fixed: "Vengeance is Mine, sayeth the Lord" -> "saith the Lord" (17834). Punctuation only: "aware of...when" ->
# "aware of... when" (psalms-54).

=== Introduction
--- The Hunted King [title]
overline: The Psalms of David's Flight · Part 2 of 2
heading: The Hunted King
sub: Again and again through Psalms we see the same pattern play out.
refs: 1 Samuel 23:19; Psalm 54:4; 1 Samuel 24:12; Psalm 57:1
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

--- Where we are
kicker: The Psalms of David's Flight
heading: Part 2: The hunted king
tile: Psalm 59 | The window · 1 Samuel 19
tile: Psalms 56, 34 | Gath · 1 Samuel 21
tile: Psalm 52 | Nob: Doeg · 1 Samuel 21–22
tile: Psalm 54 | **Ziph** · 1 Samuel 23
tile: Psalm 57 | **The cave at Engedi** · 1 Samuel 24
p: David is threatened daily, by enemies and betrayers within the Kingdom, and without. By Saul and his armies, turncoats and opportunistic men.

=== Ziph · Psalm 54
--- Doth not David hide himself with us?
kicker: 1 Samuel 23 · Ziph
scripture: 1 Samuel 23:19-20 | mark: Doth not David hide himself with us
p: Shortly after making his refuge in the woods of Ziph, Saul's son Jonathan comes to David and assures his beloved friend that his father will not find him. [[1 Samuel 23:14-18]]
p: The Ziphites and Saul pursue David, but he makes his escape to Maon. [[1 Samuel 23:24-28]]
note: About 2 minutes in

--- Strangers are risen up against me
kicker: Psalm 54 · Ziph
big: Psalm 54:3 | mark: strangers are risen up against me
p: Psalm 54 is another prayerful song that was penned by David during his flight from Saul. We can sense the urgency of David's prayer to God, with the plaintive Save me, and Hear my prayer. [[Psalm 54:1-2]]
p: There are times in our life when the relative safety we have is suddenly threatened. When even strangers rise up against us, and we have unexpected enemies that we weren't even aware of... when in desperation we should turn to God.

--- God is mine helper
kicker: Psalm 54 · Ziph
big: Psalm 54:4
p: Behold, God is my helper. This simple truth is reassuring in our time of trouble. And blessing on those who have our backs, who give us comfort and support, should be in our prayers as well. David says, the Lord is with them that uphold my soul.
p: In the Psalm, we see David praising those that help him, (mirroring King Saul's blessing upon the Ziphites for revealing David's hiding spot. The irony here is interesting. Especially given the context). [[1 Samuel 23:21]]

--- He hath delivered me out of all trouble
kicker: Psalm 54 · Ziph
big: Psalm 54:7
p: This sudden threat rising against the future King, God's anointed, troubles him enough to bring him to call for God's mercy, to deliver him with His strength, to reward his enemies for the evil they sought to do to him. To bless those that stand with him.
p: What a model for believers to have, so when our fear rises, we have the assurance of this Psalm to guide us in prayer, along with the results; God delivers us from all trouble, He takes vengeance on our enemies for us. All that we may give praise to His holy name.

=== Engedi · Psalm 57
--- In the sides of the cave
kicker: 1 Samuel 24 · Engedi
scripture: 1 Samuel 24:3-4 | mark: David and his men remained in the sides of the cave; cut off the skirt of Saul’s robe privily
p: This is the context of Psalm 57. David is among lions, physically and metaphorically. The mighty men with him, armed and dangerous. Encouraging David to kill Saul. [[1 Samuel 24:1-2]]
p: David has the opportunity to take matters into his own hands, to be his own avenger. To slay the King and let whatever may come of it unfold. And instead, David trusts in the Lord and does the right thing. [[1 Samuel 24:5-7]]
note: About 10 minutes in

--- The LORD judge between me and thee
kicker: 1 Samuel 24 · Engedi
scripture: 1 Samuel 24:12
p: He treats his enemy to a sign of peace. David makes known to Saul and his armies exactly the heart he has towards his King. [[1 Samuel 24:10-11]]
p: Reminding Saul of his exaggerated hatred for David, a humble flea, pleading his innocence, and appealing to God, David has negotiated the way of peace, and left it to the Lord to decide his fate. And Saul weeps. [[1 Samuel 24:14-17]]
take: The King asks only mercy for his sons when David comes into his power, and then rides away. Defeated by the truth and the faith of David, who did the right thing, and left his fate to the Lord. [[1 Samuel 24:19-22]]

--- Be merciful unto me
kicker: Psalm 57 · Engedi
big: Psalm 57:1
p: The psalmist cries out for mercy, twice pleading to God to bestow favor on him. Not grace, unmerited favor, but mercy because David has trusted in Him, making his refuge in the Lord.
p: Mercy is often said to be not receiving what you deserve, while grace is receiving what you don't deserve. God shows His mercy on us that we are not destroyed for our wickedness. Grace is then receiving eternal life and made partakers of the inheritance of Christ.
take: Note David isn't boasting. He is throwing himself at the mercy of the court. The mighty Judge.

--- They are fallen themselves
kicker: Psalm 57 · Engedi
big: Psalm 57:6
p: Saul, who is irrationally envious of David, and seeking his life has prepared a net for the younger man's steps. The King has happened upon the caves, digging a pit to capture David, but Saul himself has fallen into it. The hunter is in the snare of the hunted.
p: He has done nothing wrong to deserve what's happening, he has trusted in God, blameless before Him. Even this slight move against the King makes David humble before God. His trust and meekness is rewarded.

--- God shall send forth his mercy and his truth
kicker: Psalm 57 · Engedi
big: Psalm 57:3 | mark: God shall send forth his mercy and his truth
p: He then gives the sure hope he has, having yet to be rescued or brought out of calamity, seeing the thing far off, that God shall send forth His mercy and truth. [[Hebrews 11:1-2]]
p: The Bible tells us that hope isn't a wish, or a desire for an outcome, but the thing itself we are placing our trust in, sight unseen. [[Romans 8:24-25]] It is this good report of the faith of David, that we are taught the value of hope.

--- Be thou exalted, O God
kicker: Psalm 57 · Engedi
big: Psalm 57:5
p: Even in desperation, in danger, pleading his case to the God he's trusted, David gives Him praise. How can we not when we pray? We have all been guilty of haphazard prayer. [[Hebrews 4:15-16]]
p: And for this, David didn't praise himself. He didn't pat himself on the back for having done the right thing. He gives all credit to God. He sings unto Him among the nations. He exalts God above the heavens and His glory above the earth. [[Psalm 57:9-11]]
take: It may be easier in desperate times to remember our place, but it shouldn't be forgotten just because you're in His hand.

=== The thread through it all
--- Again and again
kicker: The Psalms of David's Flight
heading: The thread through it all
xr: Psalm 56:3 | **Trust in fear.** Fear is an opportunity to increase your faith and bring your feet to stay in His ways, to walk His paths.
xr: Psalm 59:10 | **Mercy.** His faith is that the mercy of God would be there with him, providing that means of escape.
xr: Psalm 54:5 | **Vengeance is the Lord's.** We do not need to fight a battle, but trust in Him to fight for us.
xr: Psalm 59:16 | **The morning.** Just as David found deliverance in the morning, Christ’s voluntary obedience unto death led to the ultimate morning of the resurrection.

--- Vengeance is Mine
kicker: The thread through it all
heading: He will repay
big: Psalm 54:5
p: Vengeance is Mine, saith the Lord. He will repay. We not only believe He is, and a rewarder of those that diligently seek Him, but that He is our shield and strength against our enemies. We do not need to fight a battle, but trust in Him to fight for us. [[Romans 12:19]]
p: When we look at David, especially his behavior towards Saul during this time, especially this incident, and we get the context of his prayerful posture during this time of trial, and great faith towards his Redeemer to judge righteous judgment, and deliver him from evil, we can understand why God considered David a man after His own heart.

=== What we have seen
--- What we have seen
point: The Ziphites | Behold, God is my helper. This simple truth is reassuring in our time of trouble.
point: The cave | And instead, David trusts in the Lord and does the right thing.
point: The Judge | David has negotiated the way of peace, and left it to the Lord to decide his fate.
point: The praise | Even when we do the right thing and make that decision like David, we don't boast of ourselves. We give all honor to God.

